(*"certification_schema.certification"
A **certification** is an assertion of facts. A **certification** is an incompletely modeled concept and requires population of related entity data types to form a complete certification.
*)


(*"certification_schema.certification.__note"
The role of *certification* can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize this entity, or by default, in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.
*)

(*"certification_schema.certification.name"
the <<express:support_resource_schema.label,label>> by which the **certification** is known.
*)


(*"certification_schema.certification.purpose"
an informal description of the reason why the **certification** is applied.
*)

(*"certification_schema.certification.purpose.__example"
A purpose for material certification could be the fact that certain customers demand material certification for the products that are supplied to them.
*)

(*"certification_schema.certification.kind"
the <<express:certification_schema.certification_type,certification_type>> that defines the class of **certification** that is applied.
*)


(*"certification_schema.certification_type"
A **certification_type** is the kind of <<express:certification_schema.certification,certification>> granted.
*)

(*"certification_schema.certification_type.__example"
Suppliers and manufacturers can be certified.
*)

(*"certification_schema.certification_type.description"
the <<express:support_resource_schema.text,text>> that characterizes the **certification_type**.
*)

(*"certification_schema.certification_type.description.__example"
A certification type in an example supply chain is 'supplier'.
*)

(*"certification_schema.certification_type.description.__note"
The meaning of this attribute can be defined in the annotated EXPRESS schemas that use or specialize this entity, or in an agreement of common understanding between the partners sharing this information.
*)

